course: dictionary
lesson: 1934
title: Commence means begin
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Commence is a formal word that means to begin or start. Exams, official notices and rules often use it. «The exam will commence at nine.» This means the exam starts at nine o'clock.
explain: You can commence things like work or a test. «We will commence the test now.» Begin and start mean the same thing, but commence sounds more official, so it fits in serious writing.
explain: Maths problems use commence to tell you when something starts. «The race commenced at 2:15.» Check your meaning by swapping in start: «The race started at 2:15.» If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Commence means to begin or start.
fact: Commence is a formal word, so you will find it in exams, notices and official writing.
fact: «The race commenced at 2:15.» tells you the time the race began.
fact: The other forms are commences, commenced and commencing, as in «The show commenced late».
fact: In French, à before a time means "at": «Le cours commence à neuf heures» means the lesson starts at nine (neuf heures sounds like "nuh-VUR").
exercise: «The exam will commence at nine.» What does commence mean? | *To begin | To argue | To finish | To cancel
exercise: «The race commenced at 2:15.» What does this tell us? | *It started at 2:15 | It started at 2:50 | It finished at 2:15 | It was cancelled at 2:15
exercise: Mia says, «Let's commence the test.» Which word could she use instead of commence? | Stop | Forget | Admire | *Begin
test: In «The match will commence after lunch», what does commence mean? | *To start | To eat | To rest | To end
test: Which phrase means to start work? | Finish work | Take a break | *Commence work | Forget work
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to commence? | Repeat | *Begin | Finish | Pause
